## What this changes

Linearized (Fast Web View) output can now pack objects into object streams, with cross-reference streams for both sections, as ISO 32000-2 Annex F allows for PDF 1.5. Until now `write_linearized` wrote classic tables and nothing packed, so a Fast Web View file was much larger than an ordinary full save. This follows `core.linearization` (#2773) and is stacked on it: it needs #2773 merged first, then a rebase on `main`.

**The switch** is the existing `SaveOptions::object_streams`, which already chooses the layout for `write_full`. It is on by default, so Save As with Fast Web View, `doc_save fast_web_view` and `pdfcraft-cli edit --fast-web-view` now write object streams with no other change. With it off, the classic layout is written exactly as before, numbering included.

**What stays out of object streams:**
- streams and page objects;
- the catalog;
- the `/Encrypt` dictionary and everything it references. These are read before anything can be decrypted, the same rule as `write_full` (#774).

**Each part packs its objects into object streams of its own, placed in that part.** Hint tables locate objects by file offset, and an object inside an object stream has no offset of its own. To the hint tables, an object stream is therefore one object, used by every user of any object in it. The packing keeps the parts meaningful:
- part 4: the open-document objects;
- part 6: the first page's objects, which still arrive before `/E`. Bookmarks shown at open (`/UseOutlines`) get object streams of their own, so the outline hint table names only them;
- part 7: each later page's own objects, inside that page's run;
- part 8: shared objects;
- part 9: never holds objects used by two different later pages, since that would make the stream shared. Its objects are keyed by the one later page that uses t…

## What this changes

Accessibility ▸ *Tag form fields* (`a11y.autotag_fields`, tool `accessibility_autotag_form_fields`) now works on a tagged document whatever its tag tree looks like. Until now the command had its own writer. It extended a tree only when the root's `/K` pointed straight at a `/Document` tag and the parent tree was one direct `/Nums` array, and refused everything else. That included the tree the Tags panel itself creates (`/K [Document]`). #2775 left this open as "field tagging in unusual tree layouts".

**Stacked on #2775** (`feat/a11y-tags-reading-order`). This PR uses that PR's tagging engine, so #2775 has to be merged first, then this branch rebased on `main`. Only the last commit (`M12: tag form fields in any tag-tree layout`) is new here.

**Any layout.** The command now runs on the `a11y::tagging` engine, as *Tag annotation* does, so it extends in place every layout that engine reads:
- a structure tree root stored in the catalog;
- a `/K` that is one entry, an array or an indirect array;
- several top tags with no single `/Document`;
- a parent tree that is flat or has `/Kids` and `/Limits`, with direct or indirect arrays;
- with or without `/ParentTreeNextKey`.

An untagged form still gets a new tree (`/Document` › `/Form`…), as before. Layouts the engine can't extend safely, such as a parent tree with a node listed twice, are refused with the reason and leave the file untouched.

**Where the tags go.** Each page's `/Form` tags go right after the last tag that owns content on that page, in `/Annots` order. On a page with no tagged content they go after the structure of the earlier pages (including the Form tags just added for them), else into the top tag. This is the default *Tag annotation* already uses. Before, every Form tag was appended to the end of the `/Document` tag.

