Set Page Boxes gets a **Remove white margins** checkbox, as in Acrobat, where it "Crops the page to the artwork boundary" and applies to the chosen page range ([Crop PDF pages](https://helpx.adobe.com/acrobat/using/crop-pdf-pages.html), Margin Controls). Ticking it shows the current page's content margins in the fields; OK crops every page of the range to its own content, in one undo step ("Remove white margins"). Editing a margin or Set to zero turns it off again. A long range is measured in the background like Recognize Text, with progress and Cancel (cancelling crops nothing); read-only and page-assembly restrictions are checked before it starts. Headlessly it is `page_set_box` with `remove_white_margins: true`, which returns the pages it trimmed.

The bounds come from a render of the page (2 px/pt, capped at 4 Mpx like the scan sampler, one renderer for the whole range), taking every pixel that isn't near-white, padded by one pixel so an edge that only grazes a pixel is never cut, and mapped back through `PageInfo::view_to_user`, so rotated pages and an existing crop box come out the way the viewer shows them. Measuring the render rather than the content stream lets clipping, images and Type 3 glyphs count exactly as they are drawn. Acrobat doesn't document the details, so these are my choices, happy to switch any of them:
- near-white (250/255 or lighter in every channel) counts as margin, and so does a white fill (slides and Word exports often paint a white page first). This is the direction that trims more; happy to tighten it to 254;
- layers are measured in the document's default state, not the view's: the crop is saved into the file, and others open it with those defaults;
- comments count as content, whether or not Hide all comments is on, so a crop never hides one;
- the box never grows past the current crop box (what is already cropped away stays away);
